The same appendix uses \[F(S,H,L)=L(24SH^2+4HS^2).\] Re-evaluating its sequence lengths and model dimensions, then substituting \(C_{\rm M}\), gives \[\frac{F(320,4096,32)}
 {F(48,1024,24)+F(64,4096,32)+C_{\rm M}}
=4.854298\ldots>4.\] The advertised inequality survives this correction within the paper’s approximate model; the whole-system per-instance cost changes by about \(0.5\%\). Counting a multiply-add as one operation would not explain the omitted hidden dimension and repeated evaluations. Sharing head parameters does not remove evaluations for different inputs.

For a finite nonempty candidate set \(X\), arbitrary finite costs \(d\), and fixed target probabilities \(Q(y)\), allowing the source marginal \(P\) to vary makes the minimization separable by target: \[\min_{\mu\ge0:\ \sum_x\mu(x,y)=Q(y)}
\sum_{x,y}\mu(x,y)d(x,y)
=\sum_y Q(y)\min_x d(x,y).\] The lower bound follows by replacing each \(d(x,y)\) with its minimum; assigning each column’s mass to any minimizer attains it. Thus Appendix A.5’s unconstrained solution and Algorithm 3 are valid, with a tie-breaking convention. Negative cosine need not be a mathematical metric for this finite transport minimization to be valid. Calling it a distance does not create the masking defect.

The implementation uses a standard-normal feature prior rather than the narrow prior above. For a second finite example, let \(X,U,V\) be independent standard normals, \(a=\sqrt{1-s^2}\), and \[G\text{ contains feature }X,\qquad Z=aX+sU,\qquad M=aZ+sV.\] Let \(Y\) be an independent fair binary label, \(P_f(Y\mid M)=1/2\), and \(Q=N(0,1)\). Then \(M\) is standard normal, \(G\to Z\to M\) is a Gaussian Markov chain, and \[\begin{aligned}
J&=\tfrac12\log(2s^2-s^4)=-4.258621596\ldots,\\
B&=-\log2-\tfrac12\log(2\pi e)=-2.112085714\ldots.\end{aligned}\] Again \(B>J\). Here the omitted conditional entropy is \(h(M\mid G)=-2.839683063\ldots\). The first construction handles the theorem’s arbitrary \(Q\) and outer-max notation; this second one addresses the proposed prior for the pointwise surrogate. Neither construction purports to reproduce historical learned embeddings. Their role is to test the universal probabilistic claim.

Take two clients, each with exactly 64 scalar activations: 32 values of 8 and 32 values of 12. A one-channel, \(1\times1\) spatial input has shape \(64\times1\times1\times1\); no spatial-count convention is involved. Let the initialized global buffers be mean 0 and variance 1 and the distribution parameter be 0, exactly the constructor defaults. Then \(a=1/2\), each raw mean is 10, and each raw population-denominator variance is 4. Clearing the running buffers and collecting the entire single batch stores mean 5 and variance \(5/2\). With all clients included and server averaging momentum 1, the result is \[\mu_{\rm reported}=5\ne10,\qquad
 v_{\rm reported}=\frac{320}{127}
 \ne \frac{512}{127}=v_{\rm required}.\] This is not a partial-participation, sampling, finite-precision, or stale-weight effect. The server’s default momentum 1 is also used in the main driver’s evaluation recalibration. The mean discrepancy alone is sufficient and does not depend on a choice of variance denominator. The independent script reproduces this scalar arithmetic without executing author code.

The loss in Equations (2)–(3) is consistent: \[-\log\sigma(s_p-s_n)
 =-\log\frac{e^{s_p}}{e^{s_p}+e^{s_n}}
 =D_{\rm KL}\bigl((1,0)\,\|\,\operatorname{softmax}(s_p,s_n)\bigr).\] The inspected training driver uses a two-class log-softmax with target \((1,0)\), matching this direction. No defect was found in that equivalence. A reviewer-written numerical identity check also passes.

Write \[P=\frac{7e}{7e+2},\quad Q=\frac7{7+2e},\quad
 A=\frac{P+Q}{2},\quad B=1-A,\quad c=P(1-P).\] At \(u=(h,0)\), \(M(u)=(A,B)\), with \(A\approx0.73387252\), \(B\approx0.26612748\). The neighbor weights sum to one, so Eq. (11) reduces locally to \(L(u)=\|u-M(u)\|^2\). The local derivative with respect to the current feature, specified on p. 6, holds the neighboring features fixed and gives \[g_y:=\frac{\partial L}{\partial u_y}
   =\frac{c(h-A+B)}h-2B,\qquad g_x=2(h-A).\] The \(1/h\) term comes from differentiating positive-anchor cosine normalization, not from an unbounded denoiser Jacobian.

Conditional repair. With column-vector gradients, the chain rule gives \[\left\|\nabla_{z_t}L-\alpha_t^{-1/2}\nabla_{\widehat z_0}L\right\|
 \leq\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}}
       \|J_{\epsilon_\theta}\|_{\rm op}
       \|\nabla_{\widehat z_0}L\|.\] Uniform bounds on both factors and \(\alpha_t\) bounded away from zero imply convergence as \(\alpha_t\to1\); vanishing \(J_{\epsilon_\theta}\) is unnecessary. The proof’s further claim \(\sqrt{(1-\alpha_t)/\alpha_t}\in[0,1]\) is false at \(\alpha_t=1/4\), where the value is \(\sqrt3\).

The pinned detection trainer instantiates `IntersectionOverUnion` with thresholds 0.1, 0.3 and 0.5 (lines 55–58), and feeds predictions and targets to these metrics during testing. The requirements pin TorchMetrics 1.4.0. That version’s source was newly acquired into the evidence directory and inspected without execution [Retained latex: \cite{tm}]. Its functional update replaces pairwise IoUs below the threshold with an invalid sentinel; its class-level `compute` removes sentinel entries before averaging (lines 181–194 and 210–213). With matching class labels, the statistic is therefore \[J_\tau=
 \frac{\sum_{(i,j):\,\operatorname{IoU}(P_i,G_j)\ge\tau}
       \operatorname{IoU}(P_i,G_j)}
      {\#\{(i,j):\,\operatorname{IoU}(P_i,G_j)\ge\tau\}},\] when the denominator is nonzero. It is not an instance recall and does not perform a one-to-one matching in this calculation.

For a batch of \(s\) users, the displayed Bernstein calculation contains, up to constants and the two-sided failure allocation, \[\left[
 \frac{2e^{\varepsilon_L}}{\alpha^2(e^{\varepsilon_L}-1)^2}
 +\frac{2(e^{\varepsilon_L}+1)}{3\alpha(e^{\varepsilon_L}-1)}
 \right]\log(1/\beta').\] As \(\varepsilon_L\to\infty\), its second term tends to \(2\log(1/\beta')/(3\alpha)\); it is not \(O(e^{-\varepsilon_L}\alpha^{-2}\log(1/\beta'))\) uniformly. The proof of G.6 nevertheless starts with only the latter dependence, substitutes the amplification calibration, and obtains the square root in (2.1).

Problem 2 (Major: the CE-bias proposition fails at an allowed constraint value).  Claim, assumptions, and location. Proposition 3.2, p. 5, asserts \(\beta<0\) for every \(\delta<\|q_1-q_2\|_2^2\), under Definition 3.1’s \(\delta\geq0\) and \[\min_{p_1,p_2\in\Delta^K}
 L_{\rm CE}(p_1,q_1)+L_{\rm CE}(p_2,q_2)
 \quad\text{subject to }\|p_1-p_2\|_2^2\leq\delta.\] It supplies the claimed sharp-label preference in the core CE-versus-L2 comparison. Use \(K=2\), \(q_1=(9/10,1/10)\), \(q_2=(7/10,3/10)\), and \(\delta=0\). These meet \(0.5\leq\lambda_2<\lambda_1<1\) and \(0<\|q_1-q_2\|^2=2/25\).

Proof diagnosis and checked repair. Lemma A.1, p. 17, makes a strict-negative conclusion from a factor proportional to \(-\epsilon\), while allowing \(\epsilon=0\). With \(S=\alpha_1+\alpha_2\), \(d=\alpha_1-\alpha_2\), and \[(D_1,D_2,D_3,D_4)=(2-S-\epsilon,S+\epsilon,2-S+\epsilon,S-\epsilon),\] the correctly expanded expression is \[T_{\rm CE}
 =\frac{-8\epsilon(S-1)(d-\epsilon)}{D_1D_2D_3D_4}.
 \tag{2.2}\] It vanishes at zero and is negative for \(0<\epsilon<d\). The last line of Eq. (43), p. 16, also prints two denominators with \(1-S\) instead of \(2-S\); Eq. (44) uses the correct 2. For \((\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\epsilon)=(0.9,0.7,0.1)\), the actual \(h\) in Eq. (39) equals \(-32/255\), while the printed last line of Eq. (43) equals \(-6208/1785\). Both defects were checked against the rendered original.

The same paragraph defines \(h\) as the squared Hellinger integral but claims \(\mathrm{TV}\leq h\). Under the displayed conventions, with \(\eta=1\) above, \[\mathrm{TV}=0.4,\qquad h=0.05894368065.\] Even interpreting \(h\) as the square root gives \(h=0.2427831968<0.4\). For normalized densities and a probability reference measure over \(X\), the correct general bound is \[\begin{aligned}
 \|f-g\|_1
 &\leq \left(\int(\sqrt f-\sqrt g)^2\right)^{1/2}
       \left(\int(\sqrt f+\sqrt g)^2\right)^{1/2}\\
 &\leq2\left(\int(\sqrt f-\sqrt g)^2\right)^{1/2}.\end{aligned}\] Fixing this constant/exponent does not repair the pointwise inference. Integrated control supports, for example, \[\Pr_X\!\left(\max_j|\widehat r_j(X)-r_j(X)|>a\right)
 \leq \frac{\int\sum_j|\widehat r_j(x)-r_j(x)|\,dP_X(x)}{a}.\] Uniform conditional control needs an additional argument. This is Major for the Appendix D.2 route supplying the conditional accuracy premise of the central reduction/convergence claim. The conditional theorem, if its premises are independently established, is not refuted. The separate DRF route in D.1 is not adjudicated here.

Equation reference: eq:lower. The training feature mean is \(|W_1|/2\). Write \(r=\|z\|_2\). By central symmetry of the Gaussian vector, \[\mathbb E\{|W_1|(W^\top z)_+\}
 =\tfrac12\mathbb E\{|W_1||W^\top z|\}\leq \tfrac r2.\] The inequality is Cauchy–Schwarz, using \(\mathbb E W_1^2=1\) and \(\mathbb E(W^\top z)^2=r^2\). Therefore \[\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal L_{\rm MMD}(D,\{z\})
 &=\mathbb E\bigl[|W_1|/2-(W^\top z)_+\bigr]^2\\
 &\geq \tfrac14+\tfrac12r^2-\tfrac12r
 =\tfrac18+\tfrac12(r-\tfrac12)^2. \end{aligned}\] Equality is attained at \(z=e_1/2\) and \(z=-e_1/2\). Thus Assumption 4.2 is satisfied: the full objective has global minimum \(\sqrt{\alpha}+1/8\). Nevertheless its distribution-alignment component never vanishes. For \(z=e_1/2\), every \(W_1<0\) yields a strictly positive feature-mean residual \(|W_1|/2\), an event of probability \(1/2\). The input barycenter is also different: \(D\) has mean zero and \(R\) has mean \(e_1/2\).

Using i.i.d. Bernoulli\((0.6)\) observations, \(q_G=0.5\), initial bet zero, and the paper/code ONS update \[g_s=\frac{Z_s-q_G}{1+\lambda_s(Z_s-q_G)},\qquad
 \lambda_{s+1}=\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}
 \left(\lambda_s+\frac{2}{2-\ln3}\frac{g_s}{1+\sum_{u\le s}g_u^2}\right),\] reviewer-written code enumerated all eight length-three paths. It obtained \[\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
 (Z_1,Z_2)&00&01&10&11\\ \hline
 \mathbb E[L_3\mid Z_1,Z_2]&0&0.004111592&0&-0.033979807
\end{array}\] and \(\operatorname{Cov}(L_2,L_3)=-0.004588782\). This directly verifies dependence under an alternative and shows that unconditional centering does not supply martingale differences. Boundedness of the summands alone is insufficient to justify the printed concentration step.

Equation reference: eq:repair. This counterexample concerns the derivation, not falsity of Eq. (11). In fact the latter can be established by working under \(Q\) first. Assume measurable suprema, common marginals, and \(0\le h_z\le\beta\). Under \(Q\), ordinary ghost-sample symmetrization gives \[\mathbb E_Q\Phi_j\le2\mathbb E_QR,\quad j=1,2.\] Changing one coordinate changes each \(\Phi_j\) and \(R\) by at most \(\beta/N\). The independent bounded-difference inequality and a union bound, using \(t_q=\beta\sqrt{\ln(4/q)/(2N)}\), yield, outside a \(Q\)-event of probability at most \(q\), \[\Phi_1+\Phi_2\le4R+6t_q.\] For \(\alpha>1\), \(\gamma=\alpha/(\alpha-1)\), and finite \(H_\alpha(P\Vert Q)\), Hölder’s inequality transfers any bad event \(A\): \[P(A)\le H_\alpha(P\Vert Q)^{1/\alpha}Q(A)^{1/\gamma}.\] Choose \(q=(\delta/H_\alpha^{1/\alpha})^\gamma\). The resulting bound is \[{\rm TE}\le4R+
 \sqrt{\frac{18\gamma\beta^2}{N}
 \ln\!\frac{4^{1/\gamma}H_\alpha(P\Vert Q)^{1/\alpha}}{\delta}}
 \] with \(P\)-probability at least \(1-\delta\). Since \(4^{1/\gamma}\le2^{2+1/\gamma}\), this implies the paper’s larger constant. Infinite Hellinger integral gives only a vacuous statement. This repair does not rely on the false dependent expectation inequality or certify the paper’s attribution to an external concentration theorem, which was not separately retrieved.

Lemma 4.2, p. 5, assumes only 1-Lipschitz activations and a 1-Lipschitz loss, and claims \[R(E)\le [\sqrt{(2\ln2)l}+1]BT/\sqrt N,\qquad
 T=\prod_{j=1}^l\max_i\|W_{i,j}\|_F.\] Take four identical scalar depth-two networks with \(W_1=0.01\), \(W_2=1\), and sigmoid activation \(\phi(u)=(1+e^{-u})^{-1}\). It is \(1/4\)-Lipschitz and hence meets the stated assumption. Let \(|x|\le1\), \(y\in\{-1,1\}\), and use the 1-Lipschitz hinge loss \(\max(0,1-yf(x))\). Thus \(B=1\), \(T=0.01\), and \(l=2\). Already restricting the supremum to \(x=0\) gives \(f(0)=1/2\) and \[R(E)\ge \mathbb E_\sigma\frac{S+\tfrac12|S|}{4}
 =\frac3{16}=0.1875.\] The claimed upper bound is \(0.0133255461\). The example uses nonzero weight norms and a conventional smooth activation, not a zero-norm singularity. All displayed norm and Lipschitz assumptions hold.

Supported impact: Major for the advertised statistical property of typicality. Appendix B, pp. 12–13, replaces the finite graph relation by the smoothing equation \[T\overset{D}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}B_jT_j+\rho,\] with independent identically distributed children and independent coefficients, degree, and density. It asserts a regularly varying in-degree with index \(\alpha_N>1\), and Theorem 4 states \[\Pr(T>x)\sim C_Nx^{-\alpha_N}L_N(x),\qquad
 C_N=\frac{(\mathbb EB)^{\alpha_N}}
 {1-\mathbb EN\,\mathbb E(B^{\alpha_N})}.\] The displayed constant contains no scale of the additive density \(\rho\).

At the initial point, \(g_\phi=g_\theta=2\), so \(\phi'=\theta'=4/5\). When the graph [loss] (#loss) is differentiated again, both \[D_\psi^{\rm AD}g_\phi=0,\qquad D_\psi^{\rm AD}g_\theta=0.\] All paths from the reconstruction or commitment term to \(\psi\) are stopped; the codebook term has no backward path to \(\phi,\theta\). A fully explicit way to verify this is to replace the three stopped expressions in the local graph by frozen symbols \(u,v,w\): \[\widetilde{\mathcal L}
   =[\theta(\phi+u)]^2+\alpha(\phi-v)^2+\beta(\psi-w)^2.\] Then \(\partial_\psi\partial_\phi\widetilde{\mathcal L}
=\partial_\psi\partial_\theta\widetilde{\mathcal L}=0\) identically. Consequently the inner updated parameters have zero assigned \(\psi\)-derivative. At the outer point, the direct codebook gradient is \[g_\psi^{\rm out}=2(1-\tfrac45)=\tfrac25,\] and the complete literal unrolled gradient is also \(\boxed{2/5=0.4}\).

Equation reference: eq:required. For a representation \(h\), the paper defines \(\Sigma(h)=\mathbb E[h(x)h(x)^\top]\) and an unnormalized sample Gram matrix \(\widehat K(h)\). Definition 3.3 decomposes the representation space into an eigenspace \(V\) of \(\Sigma(h)\) and its orthogonal complement. With \[\gamma=\min(\widehat\gamma,\widetilde\gamma),
 \qquad \rho=\lambda_{\min,\ne0}(\Sigma(\Pi_Vh)),\] the kernel-isotropy condition on PDF p. 4 is \[
 \left\|\frac{\widehat K(\Pi_{V^\perp}h)}{\widehat n}
       -\widehat\gamma I\right\|_{\mathrm{op}}
   =o(\gamma^2+\delta^2),\] and similarly for the other split. The remaining conditions bound representation and label magnitudes, require concentration on \(V\), small cross-split nonprincipal Gram matrices, and vanishing nonprincipal population covariance.

Equation reference: eq:paper34. Example 3.5, PDF pp. 4–5, and its explicit Gaussian specialization in Appendix C.2.1, PDF p. 29, allow \(\widetilde n=\Theta(\widehat n)\), \(d=\omega(\widehat n^2)\), \(\sigma^2=O(\widehat n)\) and \(\widehat n=\omega(k^2)\). The Gaussian covariance is \[\Sigma=\operatorname{diag}\left(I_k,\frac{\sigma^2}{d-k}I_{d-k}\right).\] Appendix C.2.1 states that these representations are \((0,\sigma^2/\widehat n,\sigma^2/\widetilde n)\)-decomposable. Its step 3, PDF p. 32, equation (34), correctly writes the nonprincipal Gram error as \[
 \frac{\sigma^2}{\widehat n}
 \left\|\frac{Z^\top Z}{d-k}-I\right\|_{\mathrm{op}}.\] The following assertion that the required conclusion follows by taking the concentration parameter \(\delta_2=\widehat n^{-1/4}\) does not verify the stronger \(o(\gamma^2)\) scale.

The other decomposability conditions do not explain the failure. Principal empirical moments and empirical label second moments concentrate by finite Gaussian moments. The normalized sample covariance is bounded. The nonprincipal population covariance is \(1/m=o(1/n)\). For independent noise matrices, \(B=\widehat\Xi^\top\widetilde\Xi/n\) satisfies \[\mathbb E\|B\|_F^2=\frac1m,\qquad
 \mathbb P\{\|B\|_{\mathrm{op}}>\varepsilon/n\}
 \le \frac{n^2}{m\varepsilon^2}\longrightarrow0.\] Thus the cross-split condition holds, whereas the sharper within-split requirement [eq:required] (#eq:required) does not.

Equation reference: eq:exactcolumn, eq:lower. For either model, write \(\Xi=[\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_n]\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}\) and \[A_n=\frac{\Xi^\top\Xi-I_n}{n},\qquad \gamma=\frac1n.\] The required conclusion is \(\|A_n\|_{\mathrm{op}}/\gamma^2\to0\) in probability. Conditionally on \(\xi_1\), the off-diagonal entries in the first column are independent with \[(A_n)_{i1}=\xi_i^\top\xi_1/n
 \sim N\left(0,\frac{\|\xi_1\|^2}{mn^2}\right),\quad i=2,\ldots,n.\] Consequently, for independent \(U_m\sim\chi_m^2/m\) and \(V_{n-1}\sim\chi_{n-1}^2\), \[
 \sum_{i=2}^n(A_n)_{i1}^2\stackrel{d}{=}\frac{U_mV_{n-1}}{mn^2}.\] The operator norm is at least any column norm. Therefore its ratio to \(\gamma^2\) is bounded below by a random variable distributed as \[
 \frac{\left(\sum_{i=2}^n(A_n)_{i1}^2\right)^{1/2}}{\gamma^2}
 \stackrel{d}{=}n\sqrt{\frac{U_mV_{n-1}}m}
 =\sqrt{U_{n^3}\frac{V_{n-1}}n}
 \xrightarrow{\mathbb P}1.\] This uses only chi-squared means and variances. In particular, \(\mathbb P\{\|A_n\|_{\mathrm{op}}/\gamma^2>1/2\}\to1\), contradicting the asserted little-\(o\) rate.

Equation reference: eq:repair. For the displayed Gaussian family with \(\sigma^2=1\) and \(k=1\), the stronger dimension condition \(m/n^3\to\infty\) restores the failed condition. Applying the Gaussian covariance bound recorded as Lemma C.3 to the transposed matrix, now with \(m\) samples and dimension \(n\), gives with probability at least \(1-2e^{-n/2}\) \[\left\|Z^\top Z/m-I\right\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le4\sqrt{n/m}+4n/m.\] Hence \[
 \frac{\|A_n\|_{\mathrm{op}}}{\gamma^2}
 \le4\sqrt{\frac{n^3}{m}}+4\frac{n^2}{m}\longrightarrow0.\] The other conditions checked above remain valid under this stronger scaling. This verifies restored decomposability for this specific Gaussian family; it does not verify a repair of the full sub-Gaussian theorem, arbitrary label distributions, or the unrestricted original parameter range.

Equation reference: eq:resolvent. Let \(A=1\) and \(\Delta=-1/2\). Both \(A\) and \(A+\Delta\) are invertible. The stated right side equals \(1/2\), but \[|(1-1/2)^{-1}-1|=1.\] The resolvent identity instead yields \[(A+\Delta)^{-1}-A^{-1}
 =-(I+A^{-1}\Delta)^{-1}A^{-1}\Delta A^{-1}.\] If \(\|A^{-1}\Delta\|_{\mathrm{op}}<1\), the Neumann series gives \[
 \|(A+\Delta)^{-1}-A^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le\frac{\|A^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2\|\Delta\|_{\mathrm{op}}}
 {1-\|A^{-1}\Delta\|_{\mathrm{op}}}.\] The scalar example attains this corrected bound.

Equation reference: eq:Cactual. Set \(d=2\). From [eq:Cgradient] (#eq:Cgradient), the two log-policy gradient rows at every state are \[g_\pm=\pm\eta u^\top P
       =\pm\eta\left(\delta u^\top+\frac16v^\top\right).\] Their action average is zero. Therefore the mean in [eq:variance] (#eq:variance) is zero and the exact variance is \[\begin{aligned}
 \operatorname{Var}(G_C)
 &=4\eta^2\left(2\delta^2+\frac16\right) \notag\\
 &=\frac1{864}+\frac{\delta^2}{72}
   =\frac{25000003}{21600000000}
   \simeq 0.0011574075463. \end{aligned}\] By contrast, the right side of [eq:claimedC] (#eq:claimedC) is \[\frac{2\cdot2^2\cdot3^2}{(1-1/2)^2}
 \left(\frac1{5000}\right)^2
 =\frac9{781250}=0.00001152.\] The actual variance is approximately \(100.4694\) times the asserted upper bound. The independent exact-arithmetic check in confirms the eigenvalues, stochasticity, positive probabilities, action average, variance and strict inequality; results are stored in .

Equation reference: eq:fast. Let \(N\) be the number of assigned tasks, \(c_i\in\{0,1\}\) the recorded correctness result, and \(s_i=T_i^{\rm ref}/T_i^{\rm gen}>0\) a valid measured speedup. The paper defines \[\operatorname{fast}_p=\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^{N}
 \mathbf1\{c_i=1,\ s_i>p\}.
 \] For \(p\ge0\), this lies in \([0,1]\), is nonincreasing in \(p\), and equals the recorded correctness fraction at \(p=0\), provided correct entries have valid positive timings. A tie \(s_i=p\) is excluded, in accordance with the strict inequality in the formal definition. The inspected `score.py` function filters to correct entries, applies \(s_i>p\), and divides by the supplied total \(N\); it does not divide by the number of correct entries.

For each run \(r\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives \[{\rm MAE}_r=\frac1n\sum_i|e_{ri}|
 \leq\sqrt{\frac1n\sum_i e_{ri}^2}
 =\sqrt{{\rm MSE}_r}.\] Averaging over the three runs and applying concavity of the square root yields \[\overline{\rm MAE}\leq\sqrt{\overline{\rm MSE}}
 =\sqrt{2.624}\simeq1.61988,\] far below the reported \(2.624\). Rounding cannot bridge the gap: even the upper rounded MSE \(2.6245\) permits only \(1.62004\), whereas the lower rounded MAE is \(2.6235\). Both entries were visually verified in the original PDF. The identical mean and standard error suggest a copied cell, but that cause is not established.

Equation reference: eq:IG. For a positive diagonal virtual-noise matrix \(\widetilde\Sigma_T\), the maximum information gain is \[
 \gamma_T(\widetilde\Sigma_T)
 =\max_{X_T\in\mathcal X^T}
 \frac12\log\det\!\left(I+\widetilde\Sigma_T^{-1/2}
                     K(X_T,X_T)\widetilde\Sigma_T^{-1/2}\right).\] Lemma 3.1 controls the maximum posterior standard deviation for maximum variance reduction, provided this information gain is sufficiently small relative to \(T\).

For one finite population partitioned exhaustively into \(S\) successes and \(F=N-S\) failures, ordinary task means necessarily obey \[N\overline n_{\mathrm{all}}
=S\overline n_{\mathrm{success}}+F\overline n_{\mathrm{failure}}.\] This is an identity of sums, with no distributional assumption. The displayed success percentage and denominator imply \(S=89\), \(F=76\). The other two means consequently imply \[\overline n_{\mathrm{all}}
=\frac{89(7.52)+76(13.43)}{165}
=\frac{14083}{1375}
=10.2421818\ldots,\] not \(11.12\). The discrepancy is \(0.8778182\). Each displayed mean rounded to two decimals has error at most \(0.005\); the combined discrepancy tolerance is \(0.01\). Rounding cannot account for the difference. The independent auxiliary check also finds a failure of the same identity in each website row; the parent independently recalculated the overall row using exact rationals. The exact parent result is ; all-row auxiliary results are .

Equation reference: eq:literal. For batch size \(B\), the paper uses normalized EEG and text embeddings \(\hat e_j,\hat l_k\) in a common space. Let \[p_{jk}=\frac{\exp(\hat e_j^\top\hat l_k/\tau)}
