For exact finite-horizon Gibbs sampling, one ordinarily uses remaining-time values and policies, with compatible boundary conditions: \[\begin{aligned}
 V_h(s)&=R(s)+\log\sum_a\exp V_{h-1}(T(s,a)),\\
 \pi_h(a\mid s)&=\exp\{R(s)+V_{h-1}(T(s,a))-V_h(s)\}.\end{aligned}\] A rollout uses decreasing \(h\), or augments the state with remaining time. Reusing a final finite-backup policy throughout a rollout generally prevents the value terms in its path probability from telescoping into the stated Gibbs density.

Equation reference: eq:phi, eq:constraint. The design is Gaussian, the response depends on one index \(U\), and \(n/p\to\delta>1\). Write \(G\) for an independent standard Gaussian. Increasing, coercive and decreasing losses define events \(\Omega_\nearrow,\Omega_\vee,\Omega_\searrow\). Assumptions 2.4–2.5 require nontriviality in both index directions, strict convexity and differentiability, integrability, positive probability of a noncoercive loss, and one-sided affine growth. The threshold is \(\delta_\infty^{-1}=\inf_t\varphi(t)\), where \[
\varphi(t)=\mathbb E\!\left[(G+tU)^2I_{\Omega_\vee}
 +(G+tU)_+^2I_{\Omega_\nearrow}
 +(G+tU)_-^2I_{\Omega_\searrow}\right].\] The infinite-dimensional constraint is \[
\mathcal G(v)=\|v\|_2-\frac{\mathbb E[Gv]}{\sqrt{1-\delta^{-1}}}\leq0.\]

Equation reference: eq:correct. Keeping the explicit nonnegative convention, the pointwise projection is \[
p_c(t)=-tU-(G+tU)_-I_{\Omega_\nearrow}
                    +(G+tU)_+I_{\Omega_\searrow}.\] The additive term is zero on \(\Omega_\vee\). Directly projecting \(G\) onto the respective half-lines/equality proves cone membership and \(\|G-p_c(t)\|_2^2=\varphi(t)\). At the null-model optimum, the corrected squared norm, correlation and squared distance are all \(1/2\); feasibility at \(\delta=2\) is restored. The same minus sign is required in the increasing-loss branch of \(\widetilde v\). The threshold [eq:phi] (#eq:phi) is unchanged because it uses squared negative parts.

For \(T\) layers, consistent indexing gives \[M=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}
\bigl[(W_{T-1}+R_{T-1})\cdots(W_{t+1}+R_{t+1})\bigr]
R_t\bigl[W_{t-1}\cdots W_0\bigr].\] Empty products are identities. This telescopes to the difference of adapted and original products, and every term has rank at most \(\operatorname{rank}R_t\). Consequently \(\operatorname{rank}M\le\sum_t r_t\), establishing Theorem 4.1’s all-input inclusion after repairing the appendix’s indexing slips. A three-layer, four-dimensional check with rank-one updates has residual \(8.2\times10^{-15}\) and difference rank 3. The proof, not that finite check, establishes the general result. The phrase about equal expressive power should be read as no loss: exact equality of the two model classes does not follow and need not hold with bottlenecks.

