The actual released trainer right-truncates the combined instruction and response. Let the limit be \(M\), the already-templated instruction length be \(I<M\), code length \(C\), and explanation length \(R\). With \(B=M-I\) available response positions, and component lengths defined to include their delimiters, the code counts retained by simple prefix truncation are \[\begin{aligned}
 C_{\rm retained,precede}&=\min(C,B),\\
 C_{\rm retained,follow}&=\min(C,\max(0,B-R)).\end{aligned}\] For \(M=4096\), \(I=1000\), \(R=2500\), and \(C=1000\), these are 1000 and 596 code tokens. These positive integer lengths satisfy the available-budget conditions; the example establishes a property of the slicing rule, not the occurrence of such a record in the dataset. The pinned README, line 30, states that the synthetic dataset is not released. The available material does not give a paired retained-token audit or a historical run-to-revision mapping. Consequently the exposure difference is not an established confound of the reported results and does not support Major or Minor impact. If all templated examples fit within \(M\), it disappears. This is explicit counterevidence to inferring a defect from the code alone. Resolution requires paired tokenized records or aggregate length/retention counts, fixed tokenizer versions, and confirmation of the historical preprocessing revision. If appreciable differential code loss occurred, a matched-code-exposure or no-truncation comparison would test its materiality. No such experiment was run here.

