 {\sum_{i\le n}\rho^{n+1-i}}
 =\rho^k\frac{1-\rho^{n-k}}{1-\rho^n}\le\rho^k,
 \qquad 0<\rho<1.\] This verifies only the repaired geometric bound, conditional on the post-change swap-distance assumptions. It does not verify coverage for the original label-dependent method. The defect is Major because it invalidates the special-purpose bound used to justify Table 3.

Equation (22)’s exact Hellinger affinity remains correct. A valid, parameter-dependent replacement is \[d_{\rm TV}(P,Q)\le\min\left\{1,
 \sqrt{2-2\frac{B((a_1+a_2)/2,n-(a_1+a_2)/2)}
 {\sqrt{B(a_1,n-a_1)B(a_2,n-a_2)}}}\right\}.\] This follows by Cauchy–Schwarz applied to \(|p-q|=|\sqrt p-\sqrt q|(\sqrt p+\sqrt q)\); it is \(0.4793803\) in the example, consistent with the exact distance. It does not recover the published \(k\)-only guarantee. This is Major for the core theoretical uncertainty bound.

Lemma 4.1 correctly expresses the change from moving an \(\alpha\) fraction of point \(g\) from \(a\) to \(b\): \[\begin{aligned}
 F(P')-F(P)={}&\alpha w_g\{D(x_g,c_b)-D(x_g,c_a)\}\\
 &-(s_a-\alpha w_g)D(c'_a,c_a)-(s_b+\alpha w_g)D(c'_b,c_b).\end{aligned}\] The weighted-mean Bregman decomposition proves this identity. Independent numerical evaluations with squared, KL and Itakura–Saito generators on positive scalar data agree with direct recomputation to within \(10^{-12}\). This finite check supplements, rather than replaces, the algebra. Inspection of the original p. 16 confirms that Lemma D.2 has the correct \(p'_{k,n}-p_{k,n}\) sign; an initially suspected extraction-related sign problem is rejected.

Equation reference: eq:exp. For \(d\) divisible by segment length \(s\) and \(|v_j|=a e^{-rj/2}\), the exact relative compression variance is \[
 \omega=\frac{1-e^{-rs}}{1-e^{-rd}}
 \left(\frac{1-e^{-rd/2}}{1-e^{-rs/2}}\right)^2-1.\] This verifies the principal geometric-series calculation in Appendix E. The unnormalized variance is \(\omega\|v\|^2\). For large \(rd\) and small \(rs\), \(\omega\sim4/(rs)-1\). The appendix explicitly restricts \(rs\leq1\); the amplitude-free wording of Lemma 3.6 should describe a relative coefficient, or explicitly retain \(\|v\|^2\). No scale-independent absolute variance follows from an assumption permitting arbitrary \(a\).

Use [eq:params] (#eq:params), \(\mu_0=N(10^5,1)\) and \(\nu_0=N(0,1)\). The constants in A.3 give \(r_{g4}=r_{h4}=48\) and do not include the initial mean. To avoid depending on ambiguous parentheses in the displayed \(\Gamma_0\), enlarge each \(\Gamma_j\) to \(100\). Every surviving displayed term is smaller than this bound: even taking the cross term outside the \(M_3\) bracket gives \(\Gamma_0\leq(3/4)\sqrt{48}<5.20\), and \(\Gamma_1=\Gamma_2=(1/2)\sqrt{48}+1/16<3.53\). The enlarged Theorem 1 remainder is \[\overline R_1=
 \frac{\lambda\{100\eta_2^2+(1-2\eta_2\tau)200\eta_1^2\}
       +100\eta_1^2}{\eta_1\tau}=4.1198.\] Replacing positive remainder constants by these larger ones only weakens the asserted upper bound.

Equation reference: eq:badlemma. First, retain the same problem and step sizes but set \(\nu_0=N(0,s)\) with \(s=10^{-4}\), and use any Gaussian \(\mu_0\). The next variance is \(s'=(1-\eta_2\tau)^2s+2\eta_2\tau=0.0100990025\). Lemma 1’s ascent inequality, p. 5, requires \[
 \frac{\tau}{2}\{s-s'+\log(s'/s)\}
 \ \geq\ \eta_2\tau^2\frac{(1-s)^2}{s}-\Gamma_2\eta_2^2.\] The left side is approximately \(1.151255\); the right side is at least \(24.98500025\) even after inflating \(\Gamma_2\) to \(100\). Thus the inequality is false with a smooth, everywhere positive density satisfying all initial assumptions. The Fisher information of this iterate is \(1/s\); LSI of the reference Gibbs distribution does not bound it or its log-density Hessian. Small moments alone do not bound these quantities either.

Equation reference: eq:weight. Condition on any fixed autoregressive context. Completing the square for the two Gaussian factors in the forward posterior yields \[\begin{aligned}
 \widetilde\mu_s&=c_s x_0+d_s x_s,&
 c_s&=\frac{\sqrt{\bar\alpha_{s-1}}\beta_s}{1-\bar\alpha_s},\\
 d_s&=\frac{\sqrt{\alpha_s}(1-\bar\alpha_{s-1})}{1-\bar\alpha_s},&
 \widetilde\beta_s&=\frac{\beta_s(1-\bar\alpha_{s-1})}{1-\bar\alpha_s}.\end{aligned}\] For completeness, the posterior precision is \(\alpha_s/\beta_s+1/(1-\bar\alpha_{s-1})\); its reciprocal is \(\widetilde\beta_s\). Multiplying this variance by the linear terms \(\sqrt{\alpha_s}x_s/\beta_s\) and \(\sqrt{\bar\alpha_{s-1}}x_0/(1-\bar\alpha_{s-1})\) gives the two coefficients above. Parameterizing the reverse mean as \(\mu_\theta=c_s\widehat x_0+d_s x_s\) and using the equal covariance assumed in the paper gives exactly \[
 D_{\rm KL}=w_s\|x_0-\widehat x_0\|^2,\qquad
 w_s=\frac{c_s^2}{2\widetilde\beta_s}
 =\frac{\bar\alpha_{s-1}\beta_s}
 {2(1-\bar\alpha_s)(1-\bar\alpha_{s-1})},\quad s\ge2.\] Thus the sum of KL terms is a weighted sum of reconstruction errors. It also does not include the separate endpoint reconstruction term, which the appendix explicitly sets aside. Even granting a missing square in (29) as a typesetting error does not restore (30).

Severity: Minor. Repair verified for the differential identity on a common smooth test domain; full theorem constants are not certified. For \(\beta=0\), set \(S=\alpha t^3f\) and \(\phi=e^{iS}\Psi\). Conjugation eliminates the gradient vector potential and gives \[i\partial_t\phi=\left[-\frac{\Delta}{2t^3}
             +(t^3+\omega t^2)f\right]\phi,
 \qquad\omega=\gamma-3\alpha.\] The Lyapunov operator becomes \(O=\tfrac12\sum_j(t^{-2}p_j+2x_j)^2+(t^2+\omega t)f\). Using \(i[p_j,f]=\partial_jf\) gives the exact multiplier \[\begin{aligned}
 \partial_tO+i[H,O]
 &= (2t+\omega)f-2(t+\omega)x^\top\nabla f\\
 &=2(t+\omega)(f-x^\top\nabla f)-\omega f.\end{aligned}\] Equation (35), page 16, instead has \((2t+\omega)(f-x^\top\nabla f)-2\omega f\). For \(d=1,f=x^4/4,t=\omega=x=1\), the correct and printed values are \(-3.25\) and \(-2.75\). A symbolic differential-operator calculation on an arbitrary test function verifies the corrected expression, including all derivative terms. The discrepancy is not an OCR artifact: the page was visually checked. The exponent \(t^2\) in the denominator of (34) also differs from \(t^{1/2}\) in (33).

Equation (7)’s query-side \(2KB\) bound follows from bounding two inner products and is not refuted by this example. Combining that valid bound with [eq:correct] (#eq:correct) gives a valid domination of the task loss by the averaged cross loss plus \(KB\) times \[M_w=\frac1N\sum_i\|x_i-x_i^*\|_2+
 \frac1{2N}\sum_i\left(\frac{\sum_{l\in S_i}e_l}{P_i}
 +\frac{\sum_{l\notin S_i}e_l}{L-P_i}\right).\] This is a changed, class-weighted matching term, not the uniformly averaged loss in equation (5). Alternatively, a larger uniform coefficient must account for the worst class imbalance. The local inequality is repaired by this calculation; no full finite-sample theorem or empirical retraining is certified.

Severity: Minor; reporting and reproducibility defects, without evidence that the historical scorer implemented the printed formulas. Appendix F.4, equations (33)/(35), puts a factor \(1/k\) in DCG but not in its ideal normalizer. Even granting a correction of the unusual printed rank-index set to an ordinary one-based predicted order, a perfect top-\(k\) list with \(k\) relevant labels gives \[\frac{k^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^k1/\log(j+1)}
 {\sum_{j=1}^k1/\log(j+1)}=\frac1k,\] not one. At \(k=5\), this caps normalized DCG at 20%, incompatible with Table 2’s 27.44 and other listed N@5 values. The literal index definition additionally orders numeric label identifiers and can give \(i=0\), hence a zero logarithm; it should be replaced by prediction rank. The usual normalized score is \[\mathrm{nDCG}@k=
 \frac{\sum_{j=1}^k y_{\pi(j)}/\log(j+1)}
 {\sum_{j=1}^{\min(k,\|y\|_0)}1/\log(j+1)},\] where \(\pi\) orders predictions and the nonempty-relevance convention is specified. Removing \(1/k\) from the numerator, or adding the same factor to the denominator, restores the perfect-ranking normalization.

For feature vector \(z\) and real/fake prototype means \(c_R,c_F\), the two-class squared-distance softmax has log-odds \[\log\frac{p(F\mid z)}{p(R\mid z)}
 =\|z-c_R\|^2-\|z-c_F\|^2
 =2z^\top(c_F-c_R)+\|c_R\|^2-\|c_F\|^2.\] Thus recomputing prototypes adapts an affine decision boundary in the learned feature space without a gradient update. It does not imply a universal separation guarantee for arbitrary new generators. The sample mean minimizes the sum of squared distances, consistent with equation (1) and the selected distance in Section 4.2.

Here is an admissible counterexample to [falseDP] (#falseDP). Take one group, \(T=K=2\), \(\alpha=\gamma=1\), and \(U_j=H\) uniform on \([0,1]\). These are probability measures on Polish spaces. Normal observation kernels with these atoms as means and fixed unit variance provide proper likelihoods if required. Let \(B=[0,1/2]\) in the local space and let \(C\) be the whole global space. Conditional on any \(V^K\), put \(p=U_j(B)=1/2\) and \(Y_t=\mathbf1_B(\psi^L_{jt})\). The \(Y_t\) are iid Bernoulli(\(p\)), independent of the Dirichlet weights. Therefore \[\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb EG_j^{T,K}(B\times C)&=p,\\
 \operatorname{Var}\{G_j^{T,K}(B\times C)\}
 &=p(1-p)\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T\pi_{jt}^{\,2}
 =\frac{p(1-p)(1+\alpha/T)}{1+\alpha}.\end{aligned}\] The claimed DP instead has variance \(p(1-p)/(1+\alpha)\), by its two-set Dirichlet marginal. The two values are respectively \(3/16\) and \(1/8\). An independent direct mixture check gives second moment \(7/16\): with probability \(1/4\) both indicators are one, and with probability \(1/2\) they differ and the relevant Beta(\(1/2,1/2\)) weight has second moment \(3/8\). This checks the same variance without using the general formula.

Write \(p=\mu(1)\), \(q=\nu(1)\), and let \(\ell\) denote log-odds. For behavior probability \(q_\beta\), \[q(p)=\operatorname{logistic}\!\left(\ell(q_\beta)-\frac{7p/6-1}{\epsilon}\right),\qquad
 g(p)=\frac{7q(p)-4}{6}.\] The projected CED step in \(p\) is a positive multiple of \(g(p)\). Choose \(q_\beta=5713/10000\), \(\bar p=1/2\), and \[\epsilon=\frac{5}{12(\ell(4/7)-\ell(q_\beta))}\simeq793.680537.\] This rational full-support behavior is realizable by finite data counts (for example, each max-action row can contain 5713 samples of the first min action and 4287 of the second). At \(\bar p\), \(q=4/7\) and \(g=0\), so the policy update is stationary. But \[F'(\bar p)=\frac{q'(\bar p)(7\bar p/6-1)}{1-\gamma}
             =\frac{5}{21\epsilon}>0.\] Thus the CED stationary point is not a stationary point or local maximum of the composed utility used by the proof. Taking rational \(q_\beta\) closer to \(4/7\) from below makes \(\epsilon\) arbitrarily large and finite. At \(p=.51\), the CED direction is approximately \(-4.19985\times10^{-6}\), while \(F'(p)\simeq2.83190\times10^{-4}>0\); a small CED step decreases this alleged ascent objective.

The likely correction is to remove the final exponent \(1/2\). Indeed, applying the preceding Hölder step with an increment estimate of the displayed order gives a polynomial of order \(d^{2\gamma+1}+|x|^{4\gamma+2}\), not its square root. This larger polynomial is still bounded, by a dimension-weighted Young inequality, by \[C\bigl(d^{5\gamma+1}+d^{-4}|x|^{10\gamma+10}\bigr),\qquad d\ge1,\ \gamma>0.\] Thus the final local-error target (170) has enough slack to absorb this correction. The unexplained final dimension simplification in (168) is not needed for this repair. Put \(\tau=s-t\le h\le1\) and \(q=(2\gamma+1)/(\gamma+1)>1\). The preceding integral estimate in (168), the growth bound (18), and the available moments give directly \[\bigl(\mathbb E|X_s-x|^{2q}\bigr)^{1/q}
 \le C\{(d^{\gamma+1}+|x|^{2\gamma+2})\tau^2+d\tau\}.\] The other Hölder factor in (169) is at most \(C(d^\gamma+|x|^{2\gamma})\). Multiplication, Young’s inequality for the mixed powers, and integration yield \[\int_t^{t+h}\mathbb E[(1+|x|^\gamma+|X_s|^\gamma)^2|X_s-x|^2],ds
 \le C(d^{2\gamma+1}+|x|^{4\gamma+2})(h^3+h^2).\] This proves the needed weaker bound under the stated local-error assumptions, without adding a one-sided assumption to Lemma F.3 or claiming its printed (168) has been proved. To see absorption explicitly, set \(z=|x|/\sqrt d\): the larger target is \(d^{5\gamma+1}(1+z^{10\gamma+10})\), whereas the repaired polynomial is \(d^{2\gamma+1}(1+z^{4\gamma+2})\), which it bounds up to a constant for \(d\ge1\). The factor \(h\) in (166) then gives (170). This verified surviving local-error argument, rather than ease of repair, is the reason Problem 1 is Minor. It does not refute Theorem 2.16.

Consider allowed real nonnegative one-row images \[x=(3,1,1,1)/3,\qquad y=(3,2,1,0)/3.\] All pixels lie in \([0,1]\). Their unitary DFTs are \[\mathcal F x=(3,1,1,1)/3,\quad
 \mathcal F y=(3,1-i,1,1+i)/3.\] Every coefficient is nonzero with positive real part, so no zero-phase or quadrant convention causes the discrepancy. The usual complex-modulus loss, and even a componentwise real/imaginary \(L^1\) interpretation, give \(2/3\). The asserted polar expression gives \[\frac{2}{3}(\sqrt2-1)+\frac{\pi}{2}
 \simeq 1.84694\ne\frac23.\] The independent script computes the transforms and both sides. Scaling both images changes the amplitude and complex losses but leaves the nonzero phase difference unchanged, showing why no fixed equivalence holds. More generally, for \(z=a e^{i\phi}\) and \(w=b e^{i\psi}\), \[|z-w|=\sqrt{(a-b)^2+2ab[1-\cos(\phi-\psi)]},\] not \(|a-b|+|\phi-\psi|\).

Appendix A, p. 15, Equation (3), replaces the two-cluster factor \(R\) in Equation (2) by \(2R/m\) and asserts that the resulting first term upper bounds \(I_1\). For consistent units, divide the paper’s \(I_1\) by \(8/N\): Equation (3) also drops this common factor from the spatial-correlation term. Write \[\begin{aligned}
 J&=\frac{NI_1}{8}=\sum_{j\ne k}\sum_{i\in C_j}\sum_{i'\in\partial C_k}
 \Sigma_{ii'}\mathbf1(\mathcal N_{i'}\cap C_j\ne\varnothing),\\
 U&=\frac{2R}{m}\sum_{j<k}\sum_{i\in C_j,i'\in C_k}\Sigma^+_{ii'}W_{ii'}.\end{aligned}\] The claimed bound would require \(J\le U\).

Equation reference: eq:b2. Problem 1 (Major: the stated total-variation bound is false).  Appendix B, Lemma B.2, equation (29), bounds the output distributions for \(g^\gamma\) and \(g^0\) by \[
 \frac{\pi\gamma\sqrt T}{4}
 +\frac{((1-r_f)\gamma-r_f)_+}{2r_f}(e^\epsilon-1+\delta).\] This fails for an algorithm satisfying even pure \(\epsilon\)-unlearning and belonging to the stated first-order algorithm class at \(T=1\).

The theorem defines \(r_{i,j}=\eta_i^*\mathbf1[\eta_j>\eta_i^*]+\eta_j(1-\mathbf1[\eta_j>\eta_i^*])\), which is exactly \(\min(\eta_i^*,\eta_j)\), including ties. Consequently, \[\begin{aligned}
\min(r_{1,1},r_{2,2})&=\min(\eta_1^*,\eta_1,\eta_2^*,\eta_2),\\
\min(r_{2,1},r_{1,2})&=\min(\eta_2^*,\eta_1,\eta_1^*,\eta_2).\end{aligned}\] The two quantities are identical. Their difference, which multiplies \(P_{\rm head}\) in Eq. (7), is identically zero. The lower bound therefore reduces to \[P_{\rm token}\ge \lambda\min(\eta_1^*,\eta_2^*,\eta_1,\eta_2).\] For example, \((\eta_1^*,\eta_2^*,\eta_1,\eta_2)=(0.02,0.8,0.01,1)\) gives \((r_{1,1},r_{2,2},r_{2,1},r_{1,2})=(0.01,0.8,0.01,0.02)\) and the same lower bound for every classification accuracy. The independent script checks the cancellation on 10,000 random ratio quadruples; the preceding algebra establishes it for all ratios.

With cosine \(c\in[-1,1]\) and \(\kappa\ge0\), Equation (2) gives \[s_\kappa(c)=\frac{1+c}{2[1+\kappa(1-c)]}.\] The following paragraph says that decreasing \(\kappa\) makes the function more concentrated around nearby features. Direct differentiation and a half-height calculation give \[\frac{\partial s_\kappa(c)}{\partial\kappa}
 =-\frac{1-c^2}{2[1+\kappa(1-c)]^2},\qquad
 s_\kappa(c)\ge\tfrac12\ \Longleftrightarrow\ c\ge\frac{\kappa}{1+\kappa}.\] The derivative is strictly negative for \(-1<c<1\), while \(s_\kappa(1)=1\). Thus increasing \(\kappa\) narrows the region above half the peak. At \(\kappa=1/64\) its cosine threshold is \(1/65\), whereas at \(\kappa=64\) it is \(64/65\). An independent finite-difference check at \((c,\kappa)=(.3,4)\) agrees with the analytic derivative to \(2.1\times10^{-11}\).

The distinction is material: lists of \((p,L(p))\) compare first by the binary path \(p\). An independent enumeration of the 4,862 valid paths to \((10,9)\) supplies a finite witness. Encode east by 1 and north by 0, and set \[\begin{aligned}
 p&=1101010101011001100,\\
 q&=1101010101011010010.\end{aligned}\] Both remain strictly below \(y=x\) at intermediate integer points and hence below the endpoint diagonal \(y=9x/10\). Each lies in a singleton \((D,q)\) group in the independent enumeration. They satisfy \(p<q\) lexicographically, but \[\begin{aligned}
 L(p)^2&=24668154583661/2459580573025,\\
 L(q)^2&=499765652882717/55353495840049,\end{aligned}\] so \(L(p)\approx3.16692518>L(q)\approx3.00476613\). Singleton groups make this relative-order reversal independent of the enumeration order of paths within a group.
